so you've heard in the morning a lot of  the epidemiology about this you've heard  the national and local data you've heard  about what's been happening and in  Connecticut you've heard some really  really interesting innovative ideas of  what can be done through technology and  online and now you're going to hear more  about specific effective behavioral  interventions for men who have sex with  men game and bisexual men the way this  is set up first Gina D'Angelo from the  Department of Public Health is going to  talk about selecting effective  behavioral interventions and then she  will provide a little bit of an overview  for each of the interventions that we're  going to be talking about from a  theoretical perspective the core  elements of that intervention sort of  some of the science behind the  intervention and then you're going to  hear from people who have experience on  the ground level implementing these  interventions so on that note welcome  Gina D'Angelo  and I'm short so the mic goes down okay  so on that note we are going to spend  the majority of this afternoon really  starting to look at what some of these  effective interventions are but prior to  doing that into delving into the  specific interventions we wanted to give  you some things to think about  especially for people out there who are  doing the work with MSM to think about  well how do you even go about choosing  or selecting an intervention that is  right for you and so we are specifically  talking about selecting for men who have  sex with men but it does apply to all  the other populations that you work with  as well so just for anyone in the room  who is not fully up on what behavioral  interventions are you know we're talking  about interventions are intervening in  such a way that we're looking at  changing people's behavior and the  intervention is the way that they do  that is they actually try to influence  behavioral determinants and a behavioral  determinant is something that actually  makes people act or behave in the way  that they do and a lot of times the  interventions are trying to change  people's knowledge sometimes it's their  attitudes sometimes it's the skills that  they have around protecting themselves  or not protecting themselves and so  those are the things that we're trying  to effect change on the CDC has  identified 69 effective behavioral  interventions and what that means is  they've been rigorously studied through  research and have been proven effective  at changing behavior and if you look at  the actual research studies they've  really a change of behavior  significantly we're talking about  significant decreases in risky behavior  we're talking about significant  increases in condom use and all they  have to show all positive outcomes in  order to be considered effective so  those are the abbeys the effective  behavioral interventions we call them  dearly of ease and then a lot of you may  have heard about debbie and basically  debbie that's just a term for diffusing  or getting the interventions out into  the community so CDC created something  called the Debbie project they took 26  of their 69 interventions and stuck them  in the W project  and what the Debbie project did was they  just packaged him into user friendly  kits that organizations like you know  some of you that are here today could  just take and implement really easily  and they also come with training and  with capacity building and technical  assistance for people who want to use  them as well so choosing an epi or a  Debbie which best matches not only your  community but your agency is more likely  to be effective in the real world and to  work it affecting behavior change so how  do we go about selecting an intervention  really we would like people to think  about answering two questions the first  is this the best intervention for my  target population and the second is does  your agency or yourself have the  resources to not only implement the  intervention but to maintain it over  time so how do we get those answers well  there are five simple steps to selecting  an intervention the first being a  community assessment and I'm going to  talk about that in a minute because  there's lots of different community  assessments there's large scale they're  small scales so what are we really  talking about what do you have to do  when you're looking to select an  intervention choosing your target  population and your risk behavior you  know in this case we're talking about  gay men and other men who have sex with  men today but whatever your target  population is examining the  interventions for the chosen population  and really getting to know what they are  what makes them tick to have a good  understanding as to whether or not you  can do them and that leads into your  assessing your own capacity which is  your ability to be able to actually do  them and then selecting a matching  intervention which means you want to  have a good fit between does the  intervention work for the community and  does it work for our agency so if we go  back to step one that community  assessment process you're really looking  at assessing the community's HIV  prevention needs through observations  and interviews very minor data  collection which pretty much means you  know look at the data that your  surveillance you know the DPH  surveillance already has look at some  data and information that's been  collected through your community  planning groups needs assessments that's  the kind of data that you could use to  backup your case but what we're really  looking at when  talk about community assessment to  select an intervention is get to know  the community get out of your office go  out to where people are now when we're  talking about gay men and men who have  sex with men we're talking about the  book stores here in Connecticut and let  me just tell you I had the most  eye-opening experience of my life going  to an adult bookstore at twelve o'clock  noon on a work day one of the best  trainings I ever went to was for an  intervention called community promise  and my colleagues are probably really  sticky here in the story but it really  impacted me after having worked in this  field for so long to be thrust into this  situation where you're forced to look  and see what's going on I mean at this  adult bookstore there were men of every  race age social status going in and out  of there like a revolving door and  another place that they send us which  was really interesting was Bushnell Park  in Hartford and you would think god what  are you going to look and see happening  there oh my god you know we had  conversations with the garbage men about  where they were picking up the condoms  the vendors that sell food talk to him  they could tell you when the cars come  around pick up the young men and when  they drop them back off so there's so  much you can learn by just going out and  putting your finger on the pulse of a  community so you got to find out what's  the population but not just the  population what's the subpopulation you  know there's a lot of diversity within a  group of gay men or a group of em men  who have sex with men so you might be  looking at men who have sex with men  that are young black MSM so you have to  really think about who's out there what  are they doing that's putting them at  risk what are those specific risk  behaviors that you want to change and  what are their specific needs so I kind  of went over some of this but community  assessment means going out to those bars  cruising areas parties bookstores  mapping out the activity they actually  had a straw a picture when we went of  the park and like put on there what we  were seeing and when things were  happening finding out about the activity  where it's happening and when and how  interviewing people that see the  activity like  those garbage men and those vendors but  also interviewing people who work with  them so that means collaborating with  other folks that see the same people and  talking to people who are a member of  the community itself so it's all an  effort to get closer to that core group  what community assessment in this sense  of the word does not really mean is you  don't have to do a lot of data  collection you don't have to conduct a  big old needs assessment you don't have  to spend a lot of time money or  resources just to go out and try to get  do some good information gathering about  your target population so step 2 based  on whatever you find out in that  community assessment is to choose your  population and your behavior to target  what is the main target population the  subpopulation who in what behavior are  you specifically looking to target the  guy went through all that so once you  know the pop and the the target the risk  behavior you really need to start  examining the interventions and for a  good fit which ones specifically target  your populations are very geared for  specific things and there's information  on the eby's on the CDC website one page  fact sheets that you can assess and  there's information on the Debbie's at  effective interventions org and pretty  comprehensive stuff so once you've  chosen the ebi then you need to say hey  can our agency really do this and start  looking at your capacity and what  capacity really means is you know do we  have the ability do we have the right  staff this is such a key thing you have  to have a staff person that has the  ability to go out and socially reach the  people that you need them to reach do  they have the right expertise you know  funding a lot of times we hear from  people is an issue we've got funding  please apply for it people are not  applying to do work with this population  in Connecticut for some reason we need  people to apply materials can you get  ahold of them there's some of the eby's  that online when you look at it it says  the materials aren't really available  you got to make sure that you get it you  can get them or you may have to call  somebody to get them and then finally  does the agency have the ability to not  only implement but as I said sustain the  intervention over time the  great thing about the W website is they  have agency readiness tools so you could  kind of do like a self quiz and find out  if your agency is actually has what it  takes to implement and then you're going  to select your intervention based on the  community assessment the assessment of  your own agency what's the best fit here  and then go ahead and create that  detailed implementation plan and propose  an intervention and then prepare your  agency to implement and maintain it over  time so interventions on the community  basically we're looking for  organizations to propose working with  the population but take everything into  account take into account the data from  your Surveillance Unit take into account  the priority populations from your  planning group identified the  populations that you want to reach and  but also be prepared to demonstrate the  need for them to have the service and  don't forget that you should really  think about doing an updated community  type assessment on an ongoing basis with  all your populations because things  change and people don't always stay  where they were before people don't  always act the same way they did before  dph is role we put out a request for  proposals every three years for  prevention services and we do base that  on the Community Planning Group  recommendations of what populations and  what interventions should be looked at  we do support the use of evidence-based  interventions for all populations  including MSM and gay men we have staff  that are assigned as intervention  specialists to oversee each intervention  that we fund and then they can also  identify capacity-building assistance  needs for people and training needs so  our future goals continue to fund  appropriate science-based prevention  interventions for all of our populations  including MSM a lot of people for years  have said why why we only do w zebys  well number one that's what CDC pays us  to do but number two they're  cost-effective the extensive research on  them has already been done so they're  easy to replicate they're easy to  monitor and they've you know been  researched and to be culturally  appropriate which is really important  and then we need to ensure that target  populations  identified our a high priority ensure  that our chosen interventions are those  that will have the greatest impact  because resources are scarce and we need  to make sure most importantly I think  that there are more interventions  available for MSM and gay men within the  state of Connecticut ok so our immediate  next steps is our community planning  group is right now in the priority  setting process to rank part target  populations in need of prevention  services dph will be going out sometime  around the fall of 2010 for prevention  services and then we'll be hoping to  have more organizations responding there  are your effective interventions org  websites and your dph website if you  want to know what's funded now
 

